  <h1>The Clarity Snapshot<span class="thin">One real situation in. A structured representation out.</span></h1>

  <p class="lede" style="margin-top:14mm;">A self-run instrument from <strong>Seipsum — Structured Cognitive Systems</strong>.</p>

  <p style="margin-top:8mm; color:var(--muted);">Complete in one sitting. Approximately five minutes. No preparation needed — one situation that currently feels complex is enough.</p>

  <div class="footer-mark">
    <span>Seipsum — Structured Cognitive Systems</span>
    <span>Seipsum - All rights reserved</span>
    <span>contact@seipsum.org</span>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- PAGE 2 — WHAT THIS IS -->
<section class="page">
  <div class="brandbar">
    <span class="wordmark" style="font-size:10pt;">THE CLARITY SNAPSHOT</span>
    <span style="font-size:9pt; color:var(--muted);">What This Is</span>
  </div>

  <h2 style="margin-top:10mm;">What This Is</h2>

  <p class="lede">The Clarity Snapshot is a compressed representation of one real situation, produced by you, in one sitting. It is the same operation Seipsum runs in a session — here performed by you, on your own material.</p>

  <p>It works with two variables: <strong>real complexity</strong> (how many relevant elements the situation actually contains) and <strong>perceived complexity</strong> (how complex the situation feels before the elements are separated). The distance between the two is what the snapshot shows.</p>

  <p>It is not an assessment of you. It is not a test with a score. It does not produce advice — it produces a representation.</p>

  <h3>What you need</h3>
  <p>One situation that currently feels complex. A decision, a difficulty, or a problem — real, present, and unresolved (for coherence).</p>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div>
      <h3>Real complexity</h3>
      <p style="font-size:9.5pt; color:var(--muted);">The relevant elements the situation actually contains. It rarely changes in five minutes.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Perceived complexity</h3>
      <p style="font-size:9.5pt; color:var(--muted);">How complex the situation feels before the elements are separated. This is what the two ratings measure.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="footer-mark">
    <span>Seipsum — Structured Cognitive Systems</span>
    <span>Seipsum - All rights reserved</span>
    <span>Page 2</span>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- PAGE 3 — THE SHEET -->
<section class="page">
  <div class="brandbar">
    <span class="wordmark" style="font-size:10pt;">THE CLARITY SNAPSHOT</span>
    <span style="font-size:9pt; color:var(--muted);">The Sheet</span>
  </div>

  <h2 style="margin-top:8mm;">The Sheet</h2>

  <p class="beat-label">A — THE SITUATION</p>
  <p style="font-size:9.5pt;">Describe the situation as it currently presents itself. Two or three sentences. Do not edit it; write it the way it currently feels.</p>
  <div class="writebox">
    <div class="ruled"></div><div class="ruled"></div><div class="ruled"></div>
  </div>

  <p class="beat-label" style="margin-top:6mm;">B — FIRST RATING · PERCEIVED COMPLEXITY</p>
  <div class="rating">
    <label><span class="box"></span>Low</label>
    <label><span class="box"></span>Average</label>
    <label><span class="box"></span>High</label>
  </div>

  <p class="beat-label" style="margin-top:6mm;">C — THE ELEMENTS</p>
  <p style="font-size:9.5pt;">List what the situation contains. One line per element. Do not sort them yet.</p>

  <p class="beat-label" style="margin-top:6mm;">D — THE SEPARATION</p>
  <p style="font-size:9.5pt;">Mark each element: <strong>Relevant</strong> (it changes what the situation is) or <strong>Irrelevant</strong> (it is present, but it does not change what the situation is).</p>

  <table class="elements-table">
    <tr><th>Element</th><th>Relevant</th><th>Irrelevant</th></tr>
    <tr><td class="c1"></td><td class="c2"></td><td class="c3"></td></tr>
    <tr><td class="c1"></td><td class="c2"></td><td class="c3"></td></tr>
    <tr><td class="c1"></td><td class="c2"></td><td class="c3"></td></tr>
    <tr><td class="c1"></td><td class="c2"></td><td class="c3"></td></tr>
    <tr><td class="c1"></td><td class="c2"></td><td class="c3"></td></tr>
    <tr><td class="c1"></td><td class="c2"></td><td class="c3"></td></tr>
    <tr><td class="c1"></td><td class="c2"></td><td class="c3"></td></tr>
    <tr><td class="c1"></td><td class="c2"></td><td class="c3"></td></tr>
  </table>

  <div class="footer-mark">
    <span>Seipsum — Structured Cognitive Systems</span>
    <span>Seipsum - All rights reserved</span>
    <span>Page 3</span>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- PAGE 4 — THE REPRESENTATION + READING THE RESULT -->
<section class="page">
  <div class="brandbar">
    <span class="wordmark" style="font-size:10pt;">THE CLARITY SNAPSHOT</span>
    <span style="font-size:9pt; color:var(--muted);">Representation &amp; Result</span>
  </div>

  <h2 style="margin-top:8mm;">The Representation</h2>

  <p class="beat-label">E — THE REPRESENTATION</p>
  <p style="font-size:9.5pt;">Rewrite the situation using only the relevant elements. One or two sentences. This is the compressed representation.</p>
  <div class="writebox tall">
    <div class="ruled"></div><div class="ruled"></div><div class="ruled"></div><div class="ruled"></div>
  </div>

  <p class="beat-label" style="margin-top:6mm;">F — SECOND RATING · PERCEIVED COMPLEXITY</p>
  <div class="rating">
    <label><span class="box"></span>Low</label>
    <label><span class="box"></span>Average</label>
    <label><span class="box"></span>High</label>
  </div>

  <h2 style="margin-top:12mm;">Reading the Result</h2>

  <p>Compare B and F.</p>

  <p>If the second rating is lower: some of what the situation was carrying was noise, not signal. The situation itself did not change — the representation of it did. Real complexity rarely drops in five minutes; perceived complexity usually does, once the elements are separated.</p>

  <p>If the second rating is the same: the situation is genuinely dense. This is a different result, not a lesser one — it shows the complexity is real, and points to the kind of structured work a session is built for.</p>

  <p>Either way, the snapshot does not resolve the situation. It differentiates it — and differentiation is the step before resolution.</p>

  <div class="footer-mark">
    <span>Seipsum — Structured Cognitive Systems</span>
    <span>Seipsum - All rights reserved</span>
    <span>Page 4</span>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- PAGE 5 — FROM SNAPSHOT TO SESSION -->
